Overview In Julia, DataFrames do not have head or tail functions. Although it&rsquo;s a bit annoying because it requires an extra step compared to other languages, you can use the first and last functions. Why does DataFrames.jl stubbornly ignore industry conventions? This can roughly be summarized as follows1: :
There&rsquo;s already a Base.tail function in Base, and they want to avoid conflicts. first and last were already implemented for the same purpose before head and tail. It&rsquo;s preferable to keep function names as concise as possible, and head or tail are too common and short. Code using DataFrames df = DataFrame(reshape(1:100, :, 5), :auto) first julia&gt; first(df, 3) 3×5 DataFrame Row │ x1 x2 x3 x4 x5 │ Int64 Int64 Int64 Int64 Int64 ─────┼─────────────────────────────────── 1 │ 1 21 41 61 81 2 │ 2 22 42 62 82 3 │ 3 23 43 63 83 last julia&gt; last(df, 10) 10×5 DataFrame Row │ x1 x2 x3 x4 x5 │ Int64 Int64 Int64 Int64 Int64 ─────┼─────────────────────────────────── 1 │ 11 31 51 71 91 2 │ 12 32 52 72 92 3 │ 13 33 53 73 93 4 │ 14 34 54 74 94 5 │ 15 35 55 75 95 6 │ 16 36 56 76 96 7 │ 17 37 57 77 97 8 │ 18 38 58 78 98 9 │ 19 39 59 79 99 10 │ 20 40 60 80 100 If the second argument is omitted, it defaults to showing just one row.
