Definition1 $n \times n$ For a matrix $A$, if there exists a positive integer $k$ that satisfies $A^{k} = O$, then we call $A$ a nilpotent matrix. In this case, $O$ is the zero matrix of $n \times n$.
Explanation &ldquo;Nil&rdquo; means &lsquo;zero&rsquo; or &rsquo;none.&rsquo; &ldquo;Potent&rdquo; means &lsquo;powerful,&rsquo; and is the root of the word &ldquo;potential.&rdquo; Therefore, the term &ldquo;nilpotent&rdquo; can be understood as &lsquo;having the potential/power to become $0$.&rsquo; In mathematics, &lsquo;冪&rsquo; denotes exponentiation and &lsquo;零&rsquo; denotes the number $0$. Thus, the word &ldquo;nilpotent&rdquo; literally means &lsquo;becoming zero upon exponentiation.&rsquo;
Theorems [1]: A square upper triangular matrix is nilpotent. (The converse is not true) [2]: A square matrix $A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ where all the eigenvalues are $0$ is equivalent to $A$ being a nilpotent matrix. Therefore, a nilpotent matrix does not have an inverse matrix. [3]: The trace of a nilpotent matrix $A$ is $\tr{A}=0$. Proof [1] Shown by mathematical induction.
[2] $(\implies)$ 2
Since $A$ is a square matrix, a Schur decomposition exists, and it can be represented as $A = Q T Q^{\ast}$ using some unitary matrix $Q$ and upper triangular matrix $T$. Given that all eigenvalues of $A$ are $0$, $T$ is an upper triangular matrix with all diagonal elements equal to $0$. Since an upper triangular matrix is nilpotent, $T$ is nilpotent for some $k \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $T^{k} = O$. Consequently, $A$ is also nilpotent since $A^{k} = Q T^{k} Q^{*} = O$ holds for at least $k$.
$(\impliedby)$ 3
Let us represent the zero vector as $\mathbf{0}$. $$ \exists k \in \mathbb{N} : A^{k} = O $$ For some eigenvalue $\lambda$ of the nilpotent matrix $A$ and its corresponding eigenvector $\mathbf{v}$, we can set it equal to $\lambda \mathbf{v} = A \mathbf{v}$. By repeatedly multiplying both sides by $A$, the following holds for $k$. $$ \lambda^{k} \mathbf{v} = A^{k} \mathbf{v} = O \mathbf{v} = \mathbf{0} $$ Since this must hold for all eigenvectors $\mathbf{v} \ne \mathbf{0}$, all eigenvalues of $A$ must be $\lambda = 0$.
■
[3] Proof omitted4.
See Also Nilpotent linear transformation Stephen H. Friedberg, Linear Algebra (4th Edition, 2002), p229&#160;&#x21a9;&#xfe0e;
