Theorem 1 A random variable $X \sim t(
u)$ that follows a t-distribution with degrees of freedom $
u &gt; 0$ is defined as $Y$, which follows an F-distribution $F (1,
u)$. $$ Y := X^{2} \sim F (1,
u) $$
Proof Via Chi-Square Distribution $X \sim t(
u)$, which follows a standard normal distribution, and $W$, which follows a chi-square distribution with degrees of freedom $
u$, are related as $$ X^{2} = \left( {{ Z } \over { \sqrt{W / 
u} }} ight)^{2} = {{ Z^{2} / 1 } \over { W / 
u }} \qquad , Z \perp W $$ and, $Z^{2}$ follows a chi-square distribution with degrees of freedom $1$. Since an F-distribution can be derived from two independent chi-square distributions, $X^{2} \sim F(1, 
u)$ follows.
■
Through Direct Deduction via Probability Density Function 2 Strategy: Direct deduction through the probability density function.
Definition of t-distribution: A t-distribution, which is a continuous probability distribution $t \left( 
u ight)$ with a probability density function as follows for degrees of freedom $
u &gt; 0$. $$ f(x) = {{ \Gamma \left( {{ 
u + 1 } \over { 2 }} ight) } \over { \sqrt{
u \pi} \Gamma \left( {{ 
u } \over { 2 }} ight) }} \left( 1 + {{ x^{2} } \over { 
u }} ight)^{- {{ 
u + 1 } \over { 2 }}} \qquad ,x \in \mathbb{R} $$
