Grammar Let the verb in the clause &ldquo;Let S V ~&rdquo; be in its base form when referring to the subject S and the verb V.
Example Sentences &ldquo;Come on! Let&rsquo;s start the killing.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Let the killing begin.&rdquo; - 복한규, former LOL pro gamer
&ldquo;Let&rsquo;s suppose the infectious contact rate is $b=0.2 (&gt;k)$.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Let us further assume that the infectious contact rate is $b=0.2 (&gt;k)$.&rdquo; 1
Description In fact, you don&rsquo;t necessarily have to look for examples in papers, as the sentence pattern &ldquo;Let X be Y.&rdquo; is most commonly found in mathematics textbooks. For instance, if you want to set $n$ as a natural number,
&ldquo;Let $n$ be a positive integer number.&rdquo; As seen in the introduced example sentences, regardless of what the subject S is, the verb is written in its base form. If there is anything to pay particular attention to in these sentences, it would be matching the number of subjects and predicates. Meanwhile, this sentence can also be written more succinctly and simply as a formula like this:
&ldquo;Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$.&rdquo; Bittihn. (2021). Stochastic effects on the dynamics of an epidemic due to population subdivision. https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0028972&#160;&#x21a9;&#xfe0e;

